1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:22,000
Who is it for? Any team running a customer-facing Jira Service Management portal: a public body subject to the European Accessibility Act, a university, a bank's service desk.
Why does it matter? A field labelled cf_hw_tag, or a field with no instructions at all, blocks a screen-reader user. The portal is the only door to the service — and almost nobody checks what that door exposes.

2
00:00:22,000 --> 00:00:32,000
Portal Accessibility Checker for JSM.
A read-only Atlassian Forge app that checks what your customer portal exposes, against WCAG 2.2 AA. Production version 2.9.0.

3
00:00:32,000 --> 00:00:48,000
This is the real app, inside the Jira administration area of a live site — recorded on 15 November 2026 against production version 2.9.0.
Jira renders this page only for a signed-in administrator: there is no public URL.

4
00:00:48,000 --> 00:01:06,000
First button: “Run the scan” reads your own site. The server checks the caller's Jira permissions before any read.
In this recording the page was opened with an API token, not an interactive Jira session, so the platform's caller identity holds no administrator permission: the app refuses, and says why. Nothing was read.

5
00:01:06,000 --> 00:01:28,000
The same engine, from the command line, with the site owner's administrator token: 15 calls, 0 refused, then the findings — 5 of them, on real content of this live site.
Two ambiguous custom fields, one label written as an internal name, and two issue titles. The KAN-4 title is the single letter “T”: a real defect, on a real ticket.

6
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:52,000
The same engine is also two tools on Atlassian's MCP server — the rovo:mcp module of the manifest.
Here a real MCP client, built on the official SDK, lists them: scan_accessibility and list_last_findings — and calls the first one on this site.

7
00:01:52,000 --> 00:02:14,000
The second tool returns the same findings, and states that the app keeps no history.
And the gate: an agent whose caller holds no administrator permission gets the refusal, and no report.

8
00:02:14,000 --> 00:02:28,000
The SKILL.md is public on GitHub: it tells a third-party agent how to use the two read-only tools — and never to claim conformance.

9
00:02:28,000 --> 00:02:44,000
The other path: “Run the demo scan (no site needed)” runs the same engine on the app's bundled fixtures: 23 findings, 9 to be checked by a human.
Two sources, one engine — not a hardcoded demo path.

10
00:02:44,000 --> 00:02:56,000
“Export the findings” produces the same list as a CSV: criterion, level, source, location, message, and whether a human has to check it.

11
00:02:56,000 --> 00:03:12,000
Portal Accessibility Checker for JSM. Production 2.9.0, read-only, Atlassian Cloud, no external service.
The code and the SKILL.md are public, under the MIT licence.
